.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1051 AM PDT Tue Sep 15 2026
(This afternoon through Wednesday)

The marine layer has returned, but it`s not uniform. A mesoscale,
low-level, cyclonic eddy has formed off the coast of San Mateo
County. This is producing easterly, offshore winds in the North Bay
and westerly, onshore winds across the Central Coast. The morning
sounding measured the marine layer at 1,200 feet deep over Oakland.
Surface-based marine layer detection shows it`s more shallow to
the north and deeper to the south. The marine layer in Sonoma
County is around 800 feet deep with near normal temperatures and
mostly clear skies. Meanwhile it`s around 2,000 feet deep across
Monterey County, where max temperatures are around 10 degree
below normal, and most of the coast was under overcast skies this
morning.

The synoptic scale pattern features a trough over the west coast and
a ridge over the SE US. At 500 mb, the trough will become cut-off
over the next 24 hours and settle somewhere over Northern
California. As a result, temperatures will be cooler than normal
today and even more so tomorrow. This low will also bring stronger
winds Wednesday afternoon, with gusts of 20-30 mph expected across
the East Bay Hills, Salinas Valley, and both the San Francisco
and Monterey Bays.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1051 AM PDT Tue Sep 15 2026
(Wednesday night through next Monday)

The cut-off low will certainly deepen the marine layer Wednesday
night, and it may provide enough lift to support some drizzle
Thursday morning. After losing upper level support, the low will
gradually weaken through the day. As a result, winds will be much
calmer Thursday afternoon. Temperatures will remain roughly 5-10
degrees below normal.

The remnants of the cut-off will gradually move off to the NE and
eventually get caught up in the jet stream flow again. We should be
on the backside of the trough by Friday, with a weak short-wave
ridge building Saturday. This feature looks short-lived as another
cut-off low approaches the coast Sunday or Monday. Temperatures
should return back to normal Fri-Sun with typical marine layer
impacts.

By next week the forecast confidence really falls apart. A few of
the deterministic global models show deep, reinforcing trough
bringing much cooler weather. The ensembles, however, reveal that
this is an outlier in the total guidance envelope. The ensemble mean
is much more benign, and there is at least an equal likelihood of
warm ridging next week. The ECMWF ensemble actually shows
anywhere from 5C to 25C 850 mb temperatures by the middle of next
week. That pretty much covers all of the historical possibilities
for this time of year. In other words, we don`t yet know what`s
going to happen next week.

